course: dictionary
lesson: 8610
title: Scraping: a word with many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings. Scraping is a good example. To scrape something means to pull something hard or sharp across a surface, often to take something off it. «Dad was scraping ice off the car window before school.» The ice is taken off the glass, so scraping means removing something by pulling across it.
explain: Scraping can also describe a sound or an injury. The noise of a chair dragged across a floor is a scraping sound. «Ruby fell in the playground and got a scraping on her knee.» Her skin was hurt by rubbing on something rough, so the word means a graze.
explain: Look at the words around scraping to choose the right meaning. «Sam only just scraped through the spelling test» has the clue «only just», so scraped through means passed by a small margin. When you are not sure, swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Scraping means pulling something hard or sharp across a surface, often to remove something.
fact: Scrapings are the small bits that come off when you scrape something.
fact: «Scraping through» a test means only just passing it, and «scraping by» means only just managing with little money.
fact: In science, scraping can collect a sample. «In 1683 Antonie van Leeuwenhoek described bacteria from scrapings of his own teeth.»
fact: Antonie van Leeuwenhoek was a Dutch cloth merchant who made tiny, powerful lenses and was among the first people to see microbes.
exercise: «Dad was scraping ice off the car window before school.» What is Dad doing? | Washing the car with soap and water | Warming the car up with the heater | *Pulling something hard across the glass to remove the ice | Painting the car a bright colour
exercise: «Antonie van Leeuwenhoek described bacteria from scrapings of his own teeth.» What are scrapings? | A kind of microbe that lives in the sea | A type of cloth from Holland | *The small bits that come off when you scrape something | Tiny lenses made of glass
exercise: «Ruby fell in the playground and got a scraping on her knee.» What does scraping mean here? | *Hurting skin by rubbing it on something rough | Singing loudly at lunchtime | Kicking a ball hard | Being very tidy and clean
test: «Sam only just scraped through the spelling test.» What does scraped through mean? | Forgot to go to school that day | *Only just managed to pass | Wrote the whole test very neatly | Got top marks easily
test: Ms Okafor says, «Our class is scraping the barrel for ideas today.» What does scraping the barrel mean? | Cleaning out the old rain barrel in the garden | Looking for the best ideas in the library | Painting a barrel with bright colours | *Using the last, worst things left
exam: «The courtiers were bowing and scraping before the king.» What does bowing and scraping mean? | Dancing happily in the palace hall | Cleaning the floor of the palace | Falling over while they walked | *Being too polite to someone important
